cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use or refer to this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "libadlib: An Independent Reconstruction of the ADLIB Behavior Runtime"
abstract: >-
  libadlib is an independent, host-agnostic reconstruction of ADLIB (Authoring and Design Language
  for Interactive Behavior), the behaviour language, compiler and runtime created by Stuart Rosen
  and Robert Duisberg at Wizbang! and used in Activision's HyperBlade (1996). It provides the
  runtime VM, a compiler (adlibc) that matches the original 1996 compiler byte for byte in its
  historical-compatibility mode, and inspection tools. This is a software artifact; it contains no
  code or data from the original game.
authors:
  - name: "The ADLIB reconstruction contributors"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-03
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/vomitselfie/libadlib"
keywords:
  - ADLIB
  - game AI
  - behaviour language
  - virtual machine
  - software preservation
  - reverse engineering
  - HyperBlade
references:
  - type: magazine-article
    title: "Bringing Life to HyperBlade"
    authors:
      - given-names: Stuart
        family-names: Rosen
      - given-names: Robert
        family-names: Duisberg
    journal: "Game Developer"
    year: 1996
    month: 8
    notes: "Aug/Sep 1996 issue, pp. 22-25. Describes ADLIB's language, authoring environment and runtime."
